- **F24-1** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:14`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:52`, `plugin/skills/coordinate/SKILL.md:39` - Within the stated scope (the host's scheduler is out of scope), `c3 watch` cannot deliver the headline saving: a wake that runs the watch command first still pays the full cached read of the context, so only the output/action side shrinks; actually skipping a wake requires a host Monitor or a push, which the brief defers to alternative 2. Trigger: Accepting the sketch on its stated economy and later measuring per-wake cost, finding it unchanged except for the action tokens. Evidence: read-code: The wake is armed and fired by the host's ScheduleWakeup (~1200 s); the host session must be running to execute any command, and every wake does small reads only - i.e. the context is re-sent (cache hit) on every wake regardless.; read-code: The sketch itself says 'The host's wake then runs this command first (a few hundred tokens)' - the wake still happens.; inferred: The host's scheduler 'can only fire or not fire', and changing it is explicitly not in scope. Verify: RC1: alternate status-first and watch-first wakes in one instrumented session and compare cached-input tokens per wake. Remedy: Restate the goal as 'one-line wakes plus an event-aligned --next hint', and schedule the Monitor/serve integration as the step that actually removes wakes; measure wake cost before and after.
- **F24-2** [major] `crates/c3/src/consult/detached.rs:274`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detach.rs:1105` - A watch that answers 'did anything change since <instant>' from file writes/mtimes alone reports 'nothing' for a detached background that was killed with its status stuck at `running` - only the pid-liveness judgement derives `died` - and for a worker that dies without writing anything; the watch must run the same judgement over open runs, not diff timestamps. Trigger: Kill -9 a detached background (or a host crash while one runs) and then run an mtime-based watch: it says 'nothing since T' while `--status` judges the run died. Evidence: read-code: judgement() imports and uses pid_alive; the judgement states include `died`, computed at read time from pid + start time + host, not from any file write.; read-code: judge() re-derives the state on every query; nothing rewrites the status file when the background dies out-of-band.; inferred: A worker dying with the host emits no stop event at all - the same class of silent loss for worker state files. Verify: RC2: kill a detached background out-of-band and compare an mtime-only scan with `c3 consult --task <t> --status`. Remedy: The watch folds detached runs through judge() (a state flip running→died counts as 'changed'), and worker death is covered by the event journal of finding #3 plus a periodic liveness check on recorded pids.
- **F24-3** [note] `crates/c3/src/consult/detach.rs:60` - An unlisted, cheaper mechanism exists: a single append-only, closed-vocabulary event journal (one line per state change: sequence number, kind, instant) that every c3 writer (write_status, the health journal, ledger appends) also appends to; the watch then answers 'since <seq>' exactly, with no mtime heuristics, and a host Monitor has one file to watch instead of a directory tree. Trigger: Building the watch on per-file mtime/`updated` comparisons and then needing cross-file ordering, debouncing and silent-death handling anyway. Evidence: read-code: write_status is the single atomic choke point every detached-state change already flows through - one journal append there covers all detached events.; inferred: The machine health file is a second machine-wide writer that could append the same journal, unifying the watch's inputs. Verify: Prototype one journal append in write_status and a `--since <seq>` read; confirm the watch answer for a scripted start/finish/die sequence matches `--status` exactly. Remedy: Adopt the journal as the watch's source of truth (mtimes become a fallback), keeping the vocabulary closed and local.
- **F24-4** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:58`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:72` - Under preference B' the DOM bridge protocol becomes the real host API: if the 'dumb' extension exposes generic DOM operations (query, click, read text), any catalogued adapter can drive any tab and any site, so the deny-by-default ruling must scope bridge operations per adapter (a capability token bound to the run and an origin allowlist per catalogue entry), not only the WASM-facing API inside C3. Trigger: A catalogued adapter granted read+compose issuing bridge calls against a tab or origin outside its vendor scope (e.g. driving a logged-in unrelated site through the same relay). Evidence: read-code: The sketch puts the deny-by-default host API around the adapter's calls and prefers 'the extension as a dumb, signed DOM bridge' - nothing constrains what the bridge itself will execute.; inferred: A content-script bridge with tab access is privileged exactly to the degree its message protocol allows; the protocol is the capability surface the adapter actually sees. Verify: Write the bridge message spec and walk each operation: does every op name its capability and its allowed origin, and is a call without a matching per-run token refused by the extension? Remedy: Carry a per-adapter, per-run capability token on every bridge message; the extension refuses ops outside the token's scope and the catalogue entry's origin allowlist.
- **F24-5** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:59`, `crates/c3/src/engines/codex.rs:9` - Draft-only mode breaks the engine contract's retry semantics: denial retry, format repair and timeout continuation are all re-sends of the prompt, each of which in draft mode requires the user to press send again, and stall/budget timers that keep running while the run awaits the user will turn every slow human into a timeout. Trigger: A draft-mode browser reply that fails output-schema validation with format_retry > 1, or a user who takes ten minutes to press send while the stall cut is armed. Evidence: read-code: The engine adapter's documented flow includes denial retry, format repair (a secondary turn) and timeout continuation - all additional turns of the same subject.; read-code: The stall cut and budget are wall-clock timers over a live process; there is no notion of pausing for an external actor.; inferred: Nothing in the sketch distinguishes 'engine is working' from 'waiting for the user's hands'. Verify: RC5: once a draft round exists, run one with a schema-invalid reply and format_retry 2 and observe whether the retry waits on the user and whether a timer fires meanwhile. Remedy: Add an `awaiting-user-send` state that suspends stall/budget clocks; retries in draft mode are recorded as needing-user rather than silently re-queued; a terminal `user-never-sent` class with an age.
